        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "9f5d01f7-5387-51c0-9d39-1256acd9e691",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Films: Roxie Hart, Chicago, Chicago Gal\n- People: William A. Wellman, Ginger Rogers, Adolphe Menjou, George Montgomery, Nigel Bruce, Phil Silvers, William Frawley, Spring Byington, Beulah Annan\n- Works: the play, the 1927 silent film, the 1975 stage musical, the 2002 movie musical\n- Concepts: adaptation, Academy Award for Best Picture\n\nFacts:\n- Roxie Hart, also known as Chicago or Chicago Gal, is a 1942 American comedy film directed by William A. Wellman.\n- Roxie Hart stars Ginger Rogers, Adolphe Menjou, George Montgomery, Nigel Bruce, Phil Silvers, William Frawley, and Spring Byington.\n- The play inspired by Beulah Annan was adapted into a 1927 silent film, a 1975 stage musical, and a 2002 movie musical titled Chicago.\n- The 1942 romantic comedy film Roxie Hart was also adapted from the play and was named for the character inspired by Beulah Annan.\n- The 2002 movie musical Chicago won the Academy Award for Best Picture.\n- Phil Silvers was an American entertainer and comedic actor born on May 11, 1911, and he was known as \"The King of Chutzpah\".\n- Phil Silvers died on November 1, 1985.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "064cf051-ef82-510c-9f1d-bcaed2440531",
        "text": "Page: Roxie Hart (film). Sentence: Roxie Hart, also known as Chicago or Chicago Gal, is a 1942 American comedy film directed by William A. Wellman and starring Ginger Rogers, Adolphe Menjou, George Montgomery, Nigel Bruce, Phil Silvers, William Frawley and Spring Byington.\nPage: Phil Silvers. Sentence: Phil Silvers (May 11, 1911 – November 1, 1985) was an American entertainer and comedic actor, known as \"The King of Chutzpah\".\nPage: Beulah Annan. Sentence: The play has been adapted into a 1927 silent film, 1975 stage musical, and 2002 movie musical (which won the Academy Award for Best Picture), all with that title, and a 1942 romantic comedy film, \"Roxie Hart\", named for the character Annan inspired.",
